Subtask 1.2: Create Your First Placement Rule
Now we'll create a basic placement rule that distributes workloads based on cluster location.

Create a namespace for our placement rule examples:
oc create namespace placement-demo
Create a basic placement rule for east coast clusters:
cat << EOF > basic-placement-rule.yaml
apiVersion: apps.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: PlacementRule
metadata:
  name: east-coast-placement
  namespace: placement-demo
spec:
  clusterSelector:
    matchLabels:
      region: east
  clusterReplicas: 1
EOF
Apply the placement rule:
oc apply -f basic-placement-rule.yaml
Verify the placement rule creation:
oc get placementrule -n placement-demo
oc describe placementrule east-coast-placement -n placement-demo
Subtask 1.3: Create Advanced Placement Rules with Multiple Criteria
Let's create more sophisticated placement rules using multiple selection criteria.

Create a placement rule with resource requirements:
cat << EOF > resource-based-placement.yaml
apiVersion: apps.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: PlacementRule
metadata:
  name: high-resource-placement
  namespace: placement-demo
spec:
  clusterSelector:
    matchExpressions:
    - key: environment
      operator: In
      values: ["production", "staging"]
    - key: node-count
      operator: Gt
      values: ["3"]
  clusterReplicas: 2
  resourceHint:
    type: cpu
    order: largest
EOF
Apply the advanced placement rule:
oc apply -f resource-based-placement.yaml
Create a placement rule for disaster recovery:
cat << EOF > dr-placement-rule.yaml
apiVersion: apps.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: PlacementRule
metadata:
  name: disaster-recovery-placement
  namespace: placement-demo
spec:
  clusterSelector:
    matchLabels:
      disaster-recovery: "enabled"
  clusterReplicas: 3
  tolerations:
  - key: "cluster-maintenance"
    operator: "Equal"
    value: "true"
    effect: "NoSchedule"
EOF
Apply the disaster recovery placement rule:
oc apply -f dr-placement-rule.yaml
Task 2: Testing and Monitoring Placement Rules
Subtask 2.1: Deploy Applications Using Placement Rules
Now we'll test our placement rules by deploying applications that use them.

Create a sample application deployment:
cat << EOF > sample-app-deployment.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: sample-web-app
  namespace: placement-demo
  labels:
    app: sample-web-app
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: sample-web-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: sample-web-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: web-server
        image: nginx:1.21
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: "64Mi"
            cpu: "250m"
          limits:
            memory: "128Mi"
            cpu: "500m"
EOF
Create an Application resource that uses our placement rule:
cat << EOF > sample-application.yaml
apiVersion: app.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Application
metadata:
  name: sample-web-application
  namespace: placement-demo
spec:
  componentKinds:
  - group: apps
    kind: Deployment
  descriptor: {}
  selector:
    matchExpressions:
    - key: app
      operator: In
      values: 
      - sample-web-app
---
apiVersion: apps.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: Subscription
metadata:
  name: sample-app-subscription
  namespace: placement-demo
spec:
  channel: placement-demo/sample-app-channel
  placement:
    placementRef:
      name: east-coast-placement
      kind: PlacementRule
EOF
Create a Channel for application distribution:
cat << EOF > sample-app-channel.yaml
apiVersion: apps.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: Channel
metadata:
  name: sample-app-channel
  namespace: placement-demo
spec:
  type: Namespace
  pathname: placement-demo
EOF
Apply all application resources:
oc apply -f sample-app-deployment.yaml
oc apply -f sample-app-channel.yaml
oc apply -f sample-application.yaml
